1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,451
This is STAT 331, video 21. It covers Lecture 11: the ANOVA decomposition, R squared, and F tests.

2
00:00:09,901 --> 00:00:16,365
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:16,815 --> 00:00:26,052
Type A: show that the total sum of squares is the regression sum of squares plus the residual sum of squares, and write the matrix forms.

4
00:00:26,502 --> 00:00:33,435
Type B: fill an ANOVA table, and compute and interpret R squared, as in practice question 1.

5
00:00:33,885 --> 00:00:40,605
Type C: the F test for the entire model, as in practice question 1 c and Tutorial 3.

6
00:00:41,055 --> 00:00:51,999
Type D: the F test for a group of covariates, or for a general linear hypothesis, C beta equals 0, as in practice questions 2 and 3.

7
00:00:52,449 --> 00:01:10,945
Section 1. First, a short recap from slides 5 to 8. The model is y equals X beta plus epsilon. The errors are multivariate normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times I. X has a column of ones and p covariates.

8
00:01:11,395 --> 00:01:31,150
We estimate beta by least squares: beta hat is X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y. sigma hat squared is e transpose e over n minus p plus 1. The standard error of beta j hat uses V j j, a diagonal entry of V.

9
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:43,547
We have two tests. The t test is for one coefficient. The F test is for a joint null hypothesis, beta star equals 0, where beta star has length q.

10
00:01:43,997 --> 00:01:58,930
In Lecture 10, indicators, interactions and polynomial terms only changed the columns of X. So all these tools still apply. This lecture adds a new way to look at the F test: the sums of squares.

11
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:15,508
Slide 15 recalls the fit. beta hat gives the fitted values y hat, which equal H times y. H is the hat matrix. The residuals e are y minus y hat, which equal I minus H, times y.

12
00:02:15,958 --> 00:02:31,531
The plot uses the mercury data and model M1, with fishpart and weight. Each blue point is one fisherman: the fitted value across, the observed MeHg up. The green line is y equals y hat.

13
00:02:31,981 --> 00:02:45,891
The variability in the outcome is how far the y i values are from their mean, y bar. The purple dashed line is y bar, 3.644. The points spread far above and below it.

14
00:02:46,341 --> 00:02:53,743
ANOVA means analysis of variance. It asks: how much of this variability does our model explain?

15
00:02:54,193 --> 00:03:05,116
Slide 16 is drawn in class. Here we draw it with the mercury data. Each point has an observed value y i and a fitted value y hat i.

16
00:03:05,566 --> 00:03:08,147
The purple line is the mean, y bar.

17
00:03:08,597 --> 00:03:18,432
Take one subject, number 84. Its total deviation is y i minus y bar: the black bar, from the purple line up to the point.

18
00:03:18,882 --> 00:03:31,255
The green bar goes from y bar up to the fitted value y hat i, on the green line. This part, y hat i minus y bar, is the part that the model explains.

19
00:03:31,705 --> 00:03:40,729
The orange bar goes from y hat i up to y i. This is the residual e i: the part that the model does not explain.

20
00:03:41,179 --> 00:03:52,315
So the total deviation is the residual plus the explained part. This holds for every subject. Next, we square these parts and add them over all subjects.

21
00:03:52,765 --> 00:04:05,757
Question type A asks you to show the decomposition: S S Total equals S S Reg plus S S Res, and to write each sum with matrices. Method card A has four steps.

22
00:04:06,207 --> 00:04:20,202
Step 1: split. Write each deviation, or the whole vector, as two parts: the residual part and the regression part. In the picture, the black arrow is the sum of the green arrow and the orange arrow.

23
00:04:20,652 --> 00:04:34,796
Step 2: square and add. For one number, a plus b squared is a squared plus b squared plus 2 a b. For a vector, multiply out a transpose a. The squared lengths of the three marked sides appear.

24
00:04:35,246 --> 00:04:52,889
Step 3: use the facts. e transpose X is 0. The ones vector is a column of X, so H times 1 is 1. H transpose H is H. And 1 transpose y is n y bar. In the picture, e is at a right angle to the plane.

25
00:04:53,339 --> 00:05:02,576
Step 4: name the sums: S S Res, S S Reg, and S S Total. Now we follow the card on slides 17 to 19.

26
00:05:03,026 --> 00:05:26,429
Here is the derivation of slide 17, with card A. Pause the video and try it. Start from the definition: S S Total is the sum of y i minus y bar, squared. For subject 84, y i is 11.58 and y bar is 3.644. The black bar is the difference, 7.936.

27
00:05:26,879 --> 00:05:44,031
Step 1 of the card: split each deviation into y i minus y hat i, plus y hat i minus y bar. For subject 84, 5.174 plus 2.762 is 7.936.

28
00:05:44,481 --> 00:06:02,507
Step 2: expand the square. a plus b, squared, is a squared plus b squared plus 2 a b. The square shows it for subject 84. The two marked rectangles are the cross term: 2 times 14.29, which is not 0 for one subject.

29
00:06:02,957 --> 00:06:20,685
Step 4: name the sums. The first sum is the residual sum of squares, S S Res. The second is the regression sum of squares, S S Reg. The cross term, added over all 135 subjects, is 0. The next slide shows why.

30
00:06:21,135 --> 00:06:31,333
So S S Total equals S S Res plus S S Reg. In the ANOVA table of type B, the S S column adds up because of this result.

31
00:06:31,783 --> 00:06:49,340
Slide 18 shows why the cross term is 0. We are at step 3 of card A: use the facts. y i minus y hat i is the residual e i. The orange arrow e goes from y hat up to y. The plane is the column space of X.

32
00:06:49,790 --> 00:07:05,790
A sum of products is a vector product: e transpose, times the vector y hat minus y bar times 1. 1 is the vector of ones. In the picture, y hat minus y bar 1 is the green arrow in the plane.

33
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:18,400
y hat is X beta hat. Multiply out: e transpose X beta hat, minus y bar times e transpose 1. X beta hat is a vector in the plane.

34
00:07:18,850 --> 00:07:33,719
From the normal equations, e transpose X is 0. Also, 1 is the first column of X, so e transpose 1 is 0. So the cross term is 0. In the picture, e is at a right angle to the plane.

35
00:07:34,169 --> 00:07:47,609
So the three arrows make a right triangle. By Pythagoras, the squared length of the black side equals the sum of the other two. That is S S Total equals S S Reg plus S S Res.

36
00:07:48,059 --> 00:08:00,945
Slide 19 writes the sums with matrices. Step 1 of card A: write the sum as a vector. S S Total is the squared length of y minus y bar times the ones vector.

37
00:08:01,395 --> 00:08:13,960
Now write y bar times 1 with a matrix. J is an n by n matrix of ones. We use a small example of our own: n is 4, and y is 2, 4, 3, 7.

38
00:08:14,410 --> 00:08:25,311
J times y: row 1 of J times y adds all the y values. 2 plus 4 plus 3 plus 7 is 16. Every row gives 16.

39
00:08:25,761 --> 00:08:40,567
Divide by n. One over n, J y, has 16 over 4, which is 4, in every entry. 4 is y bar. So one over n J y is y bar times the ones vector. This is line 2.

40
00:08:41,017 --> 00:08:45,753
Put line 2 into line 1. This is the form on slide 19.

41
00:08:46,203 --> 00:08:58,384
Step 2: multiply out line 1. The middle term is minus 2 y bar times 1 transpose y. The last term is y bar squared times 1 transpose 1.

42
00:08:58,834 --> 00:09:07,709
Step 3: use the facts. 1 transpose y is the sum of the y i, which is n y bar. 1 transpose 1 is n.

43
00:09:08,159 --> 00:09:15,241
Step 4: collect the terms. S S Total equals y transpose y minus n y bar squared.

44
00:09:15,691 --> 00:09:37,942
Check with the example. y transpose y is 4 plus 16 plus 9 plus 49, which is 78. n y bar squared is 4 times 16, which is 64. 78 minus 64 is 14. The deviations are minus 2, 0, minus 1 and 3. Their squares add to 14 too.

45
00:09:38,392 --> 00:09:59,192
Now S S Reg, with the same card. Step 1: S S Reg is the sum of y hat i minus y bar, squared. y hat is H y, and y bar 1 is one over n J y. So S S Reg is the squared length of H y minus one over n J y: the green side.

46
00:09:59,642 --> 00:10:07,002
Step 2: multiply out, in the same way as for S S Total. The marked parts change in the next line.

47
00:10:07,452 --> 00:10:23,175
Step 3: use the facts. From Lecture 6, H is symmetric and idempotent, so H transpose H is H. Also, 1 is a column of X, so H times 1 is 1, and 1 transpose H is 1 transpose.

48
00:10:23,625 --> 00:10:33,566
1 transpose y is n y bar. Collect the terms. S S Reg equals y transpose H y minus n y bar squared.

49
00:10:34,016 --> 00:10:51,488
S S Res is the squared length of y minus H y: the orange side. Multiply out with I minus H, which is symmetric and idempotent. S S Res equals y transpose, I minus H, y. Lecture 7 used this form.

50
00:10:51,938 --> 00:11:06,530
Check: S S Reg plus S S Res is y transpose y minus n y bar squared, which is S S Total. Type C uses the form of S S Reg with H minus one over n J.

51
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:25,519
Section 2. Slide 24 computes the three sums for the mercury model M1, with fishpart and weight. S S Total, the total variation of MeHg around its mean, is 1091.77. The purple bar shows it.

52
00:11:25,969 --> 00:11:33,862
The model splits this bar into two parts. S S Reg, in green, is 263.74.

53
00:11:34,312 --> 00:11:38,728
S S Res, in orange, is 828.03.

54
00:11:39,178 --> 00:11:54,261
Check: 263.74 plus 828.03 is 1091.77. The R output on slide 24 shows the same total two times.

55
00:11:54,711 --> 00:12:10,177
Slide 20 defines the coefficient of determination, R squared. It is S S Reg over S S Total. Because the two parts add to the total, it is also 1 minus S S Res over S S Total.

56
00:12:10,627 --> 00:12:33,625
For the mercury model, R squared is 263.74 over 1091.77, which is 0.2416. In the bar, it is the green part as a fraction of the whole bar. The R output on slide 24 shows Multiple R-squared, 0.2416.

57
00:12:34,075 --> 00:12:47,088
So R squared is the proportion of the variability in y that the regression model explains. Fishpart and weight explain about 24 percent of the variation in MeHg.

58
00:12:47,538 --> 00:13:09,127
Slide 20 asks how to interpret R squared equal to 1, and R squared equal to 0. Pause the video and try it. R squared is 1 when S S Res is 0. Then every residual is 0, and every point is on the line y equals y hat. The model explains all the variability.

59
00:13:09,577 --> 00:13:27,305
R squared is 0 when S S Reg is 0. Then every fitted value equals y bar: all points are above the same fitted value. The covariates explain none of the variability. Most real models, like the mercury model, are in between.

60
00:13:27,755 --> 00:13:42,433
Question type B gives an ANOVA table with some numbers, and asks for the rest, or for R squared. Slide 25 shows the table. It has a row for the regression, a row for the residuals, and a total row.

61
00:13:42,883 --> 00:13:55,811
Step 1 of method card B: the degrees of freedom. Regression has p. Residuals have n minus p plus 1. Total has n minus 1. The first two add to the total.

62
00:13:56,261 --> 00:14:05,242
Step 2: the sums of squares. S S Reg plus S S Res equals S S Total. This is the decomposition of type A.

63
00:14:05,692 --> 00:14:16,017
Step 3: MS means mean square. Each mean square is the sum of squares over its degrees of freedom. The residual mean square is sigma hat squared.

64
00:14:16,467 --> 00:14:21,395
Step 4: F is the regression mean square over the residual mean square.

65
00:14:21,845 --> 00:14:31,040
Step 5: R squared is S S Reg over S S Total: the proportion of the variability of y that the model explains.

66
00:14:31,490 --> 00:14:51,223
Here is a type B question with the mercury numbers of slides 24 and 25. Pause the video and try it. n is 135 and p is 4. R gives S S Reg, 263.74, and S S Res, 828.03.

67
00:14:51,673 --> 00:15:12,004
Step 1 of card B: the degrees of freedom. Regression has p, which is 4. Residuals have 135 minus 5, which is 130. The total is 4 plus 130, which is 134. Check: n minus 1 is also 134.

68
00:15:12,454 --> 00:15:25,915
Step 2: S S Total is 263.74 plus 828.03, which is 1091.77. The purple bar shows it.

69
00:15:26,365 --> 00:15:43,709
Step 3: the mean squares. 263.74 over 4 is 65.94. 828.03 over 130 is 6.3695. This is sigma hat squared.

70
00:15:44,159 --> 00:15:51,903
Step 4: F is 65.94 over 6.3695, which is 10.35.

71
00:15:52,353 --> 00:16:09,548
Step 5: R squared is 263.74 over 1091.77, which is 0.2416. The model explains about 24 percent of the variability of MeHg.

72
00:16:09,998 --> 00:16:17,571
Slide 24 ends with three lines of the R summary output. Now we can explain almost every number.

73
00:16:18,021 --> 00:16:41,957
Residual standard error is sigma hat: the square root of the residual mean square, step 3 of card B. That is the square root of 828.03 over 130, which is the square root of 6.3695, which is 2.524. The 130 is the residual degrees of freedom.

74
00:16:42,407 --> 00:16:53,458
Multiple R-squared is S S Reg over S S Total, step 5: 0.2416. Adjusted R squared comes in a later lecture.

75
00:16:53,908 --> 00:17:15,817
The F-statistic line is step 4: F is 65.94 over 6.3695, which is 10.35, with 4 and 130 degrees of freedom. The p-value, 2.613 times 10 to the minus 7, is the test of the entire model, in section 3.

76
00:17:16,267 --> 00:17:32,374
Practice question 1. An ANOVA table has some missing values. Part a: how many observations? Part b: what is S S Total? Part c: test that all coefficients except the intercept are 0. Pause the video and try it.

77
00:17:32,824 --> 00:17:52,024
Start with the regression degrees of freedom. Step 3 of card B says MS is S S over its degrees of freedom. So the degrees of freedom are S S over MS: 138.75 over 46.25, which is 3. So p is 3.

78
00:17:52,474 --> 00:18:14,447
Step 1: the degrees of freedom add up. The total is 3 plus 131, which is 134. The total degrees of freedom are n minus 1, so n is 135. This answers part a. Check: n minus p plus 1 is 135 minus 4, which is 131.

79
00:18:14,897 --> 00:18:29,041
Part b, step 2: the sums of squares add up. S S Total is 138.75 plus 953.02, which is 1091.77.

80
00:18:29,491 --> 00:18:45,150
Part c needs F. Step 4: F is the regression mean square over the residual mean square: 46.25 over 7.275, which is 6.36. The test is in section 3.

81
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:56,267
Section 3. Slide 21 tests the whole model. The design matrix has the column of ones and p covariate columns, marked in orange.

82
00:18:56,717 --> 00:19:16,365
The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 2 and so on, up to beta p, all equal to 0. This says: y has no association with any of the covariates. Under this null, the model is beta 0 plus epsilon, and every fitted value is y bar.

83
00:19:16,815 --> 00:19:33,433
The test uses the decomposition. F is S S Reg over p, divided by S S Res over n minus p plus 1. Under the null, F has an F distribution with p and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

84
00:19:33,883 --> 00:19:51,462
If F is large, the model explains a large part of the variation. That is evidence of an association with some of the covariates. We reject the null hypothesis when the p-value is less than alpha. A large F does not say which covariates matter.

85
00:19:51,912 --> 00:20:11,859
Slide 22 shows why. Recall: if X 1 is chi squared with nu 1 degrees of freedom, X 2 is chi squared with nu 2, and they are independent, then X 1 over nu 1, divided by X 2 over nu 2, has an F distribution. So we need three facts.

86
00:20:12,309 --> 00:20:21,290
Fact 1: under the null, S S Reg over sigma squared is chi squared with p degrees of freedom. The next slide sketches it.

87
00:20:21,740 --> 00:20:34,092
Fact 2: S S Res over sigma squared is e transpose e over sigma squared. Lecture 7 showed that it is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

88
00:20:34,542 --> 00:20:47,491
Fact 3: S S Reg and S S Res are independent. S S Reg uses y hat, which is X beta hat. S S Res uses e. And beta hat and e are independent.

89
00:20:47,941 --> 00:21:01,872
Put the two chi squared variables into the ratio. sigma squared is in the top and in the bottom, so it cancels. The result is the F statistic, with p and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

90
00:21:02,322 --> 00:21:14,247
Slide 23 sketches fact 1. Write S S Reg as y transpose, H minus one over n J, y. This is the matrix form of slide 19.

91
00:21:14,697 --> 00:21:30,612
The plan is the same as the proof in Lecture 7. Eigendecompose the matrix. It is idempotent, so its eigenvalues are 0 or 1. To count the ones, use the trace: the sum of the eigenvalues equals the trace.

92
00:21:31,062 --> 00:21:38,827
The trace of a difference is the difference of the traces: the trace of H minus the trace of one over n J.

93
00:21:39,277 --> 00:21:52,077
H is X, times X transpose X inverse, times X transpose. The diagonal of one over n J has n entries, each one over n. So its trace is 1.

94
00:21:52,527 --> 00:22:05,925
The trace does not change when we swap the two factors: the trace of A B equals the trace of B A. Take A as X. A B is n by n. B A is p plus 1 by p plus 1.

95
00:22:06,375 --> 00:22:20,924
B A is X transpose X inverse, times X transpose X, which is the identity of size p plus 1. Its trace is p plus 1. So the trace is p plus 1 minus 1, which is p.

96
00:22:21,374 --> 00:22:35,795
So exactly p eigenvalues are 1. As in Lecture 7, S S Reg over sigma squared is then chi squared with p degrees of freedom under the null. This gives the null distribution of type C.

97
00:22:36,245 --> 00:22:44,011
Question type C asks: is y associated with any of the covariates? Method card C has four steps.

98
00:22:44,461 --> 00:22:59,437
Step 1: write the null hypothesis: all p covariate coefficients are 0. Find p and the residual degrees of freedom, n minus p plus 1. They fix the null distribution, the black curve.

99
00:22:59,887 --> 00:23:14,841
Step 2: compute F, S S Reg over p, divided by S S Res over n minus p plus 1. Take the numbers from the ANOVA table, or from R squared. The blue line is the observed F.

100
00:23:15,291 --> 00:23:28,689
Step 3: under the null, F has an F distribution with p and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. The red region starts at the critical value, the 95 percent quantile.

101
00:23:29,139 --> 00:23:40,957
Step 4: the p-value is the area beyond the observed F. Reject the null hypothesis if the p-value is less than alpha. Then say the conclusion in words.

102
00:23:41,407 --> 00:24:04,405
A type C example: slide 24 tests the whole mercury model M1. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: the null says all four covariate coefficients are 0: the three fishpart indicators and weight. n is 135, and p is 4, so n minus p plus 1 is 130.

103
00:24:04,855 --> 00:24:25,100
Step 2: S S Reg over p is 263.74 over 4, which is 65.94. S S Res over 130 is 828.03 over 130, which is 6.3695.

104
00:24:25,550 --> 00:24:35,385
F is 65.94 over 6.3695, which is 10.35. The blue line is the observed F.

105
00:24:35,835 --> 00:24:51,771
Step 3: the null distribution is F with 4 and 130 degrees of freedom. The red region starts at 2.441, the 95 percent quantile. 10.35 is far into the red region.

106
00:24:52,221 --> 00:25:10,461
Step 4: the p-value is the area beyond 10.35. It is 2.6 times 10 to the minus 7, less than 0.05. We reject the null hypothesis. MeHg is associated with some of the covariates.

107
00:25:10,911 --> 00:25:29,983
Part c of practice question 1 is type C. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 2 equals beta 3 equals 0. From part a, p is 3, and the residual degrees of freedom are 131.

108
00:25:30,433 --> 00:25:41,483
Step 2: F is the regression mean square over the residual mean square: 46.25 over 7.275, which is 6.36.

109
00:25:41,933 --> 00:25:58,595
Step 3: under the null, F has an F distribution with 3 and 131 degrees of freedom. The 95 percent quantile is 2.674. 6.36 is larger, so it is in the red region.

110
00:25:59,045 --> 00:26:16,325
Step 4: the p-value is 0.000467, less than 0.05. We reject the null hypothesis at the 0.05 level. This matches the solution on slide 37.

111
00:26:16,775 --> 00:26:36,871
A tutorial question of type C. Tutorial 3, question 1. The model fit 1 has salary and the district size as a factor with 3 levels, so it has 2 indicators. Part a asks for n. Part c asks: does mean salary differ among the three sizes? Pause the video and try it.

112
00:26:37,321 --> 00:26:59,891
Step 1 of card C. The null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 2 equals 0: all coefficients except the intercept. So p is 2. The output gives 322 residual degrees of freedom. n minus 3 is 322, so n is 325. This answers part a.

113
00:27:00,341 --> 00:27:11,051
Step 2: F is S S Reg over p, divided by S S Res over n minus p plus 1. The output does not give the sums of squares.

114
00:27:11,501 --> 00:27:25,623
Divide the top and the bottom by S S Total. S S Reg over S S Total is R squared, and S S Res over S S Total is 1 minus R squared. The bar shows the two parts.

115
00:27:26,073 --> 00:27:34,223
Put in R squared, 0.3909 from the output, p equal to 2, and 322.

116
00:27:34,673 --> 00:27:56,689
0.3909 over 2 is 0.19545. 0.6091 over 322 is 0.0018916. The ratio is 103.3. This is the F-statistic line of the output.

117
00:27:57,139 --> 00:28:11,880
Step 3: the null distribution is F with 2 and 322 degrees of freedom. 103.3 is much larger than 3.024, the 95 percent quantile.

118
00:28:12,330 --> 00:28:29,439
Step 4: the p-value is less than 2.2 times 10 to the minus 16. We reject the null hypothesis. District size matters for mean salary. The posted solution uses the Lecture 9 formula, and gets the same F.

119
00:28:29,889 --> 00:28:39,681
Section 4. Slide 26 tests a group of covariates. Take the full model with three covariates, x 1, x 2 and x 3.

120
00:28:40,131 --> 00:28:54,766
Split the columns of X into two blocks. X dagger has the columns we keep, here the ones and x 1. X star has the q columns we test, here x 2 and x 3. beta splits in the same way.

121
00:28:55,216 --> 00:29:09,360
The null hypothesis is beta star equals 0. Here, beta 2 equals beta 3 equals 0, so q is 2. This is the same as leaving the columns of X star out of the model.

122
00:29:09,810 --> 00:29:24,359
So fit two models. The full model has all the columns. The reduced model has only X dagger. It is the model under the null hypothesis. Here it is beta 0 plus beta 1 x i 1.

123
00:29:24,809 --> 00:29:30,527
The question is: does the full model explain much more variability than the reduced model?

124
00:29:30,977 --> 00:29:53,697
Slide 27 compares the two fits. The bars use the mercury data: the reduced model has weight only. Both bars have the same length, S S Total, because y is the same. The reduced model has S S Reg 187.166 and S S Res 904.605.

125
00:29:54,147 --> 00:30:08,163
The full model adds the three fishpart indicators. Its S S Reg is larger, 263.742, and its S S Res is smaller, 828.030.

126
00:30:08,613 --> 00:30:33,381
The red box is the extra variation that the q added covariates explain: S S Reg of the full model, minus S S Reg of the reduced model. Here it is 76.576. By the same logic as before, under the null, this over sigma squared is chi squared with q degrees of freedom. q is the difference in the number of parameters.

127
00:30:33,831 --> 00:30:46,929
Divide by q, and divide by the residual mean square of the full model. Under the null, this F has an F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

128
00:30:47,379 --> 00:31:06,963
Slide 28 says: we saw this test before. In Lecture 9, the F statistic was beta star hat transpose, times V star inverse, times beta star hat, over q, divided by sigma hat squared. It measures how far the estimates are from the null value 0.

129
00:31:07,413 --> 00:31:14,795
The new version uses sums of squares. It measures how much extra variability the q covariates explain.

130
00:31:15,245 --> 00:31:32,717
The two F statistics are exactly equal. On slide 24, the R code computes both for the mercury data, and both give 4.007444. Assignment 3 asks why they are equal, so this video does not show it.

131
00:31:33,167 --> 00:31:54,884
Slide 31 uses the same method for a larger class of null hypotheses: general linear hypotheses. Each has the form C beta equals 0, where C is a matrix of rank r. Example: in a model with four covariates, test beta 1 equals beta 2 equals 0.

132
00:31:55,334 --> 00:32:03,355
Write one row of C for each restriction. C has 5 columns, one for each of beta 0 to beta 4.

133
00:32:03,805 --> 00:32:13,235
Row 1 is 0, 1, 0, 0, 0. Times beta, it gives beta 1. So row 1 says beta 1 equals 0.

134
00:32:13,685 --> 00:32:24,714
Row 2 is 0, 0, 1, 0, 0. It gives beta 2 equals 0. The two rows are not multiples of each other, so the rank r is 2.

135
00:32:25,164 --> 00:32:44,983
Slide 33 gives the test. Fit the full model, and the reduced model under C beta equals 0. The F statistic is the same as before, with r in place of q. Under the null, it has an F distribution with r and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

136
00:32:45,433 --> 00:32:57,998
Question type D tests several restrictions at the same time: a group of coefficients equal to 0, or any C beta equals 0. Method card D has five steps.

137
00:32:58,448 --> 00:33:11,013
Step 1: write the null hypothesis as C beta equals 0, with one row for each restriction. r is the rank of C. For a group of q coefficients, r is q.

138
00:33:11,463 --> 00:33:21,597
Step 2: the reduced model. Put the restrictions into the model. Add the columns that share a coefficient, or leave out the columns of X star.

139
00:33:22,047 --> 00:33:38,068
Step 3: fit both models. The extra sum of squares is S S Reg of the full model minus S S Reg of the reduced model. It is also S S Res of the reduced model minus S S Res of the full model.

140
00:33:38,518 --> 00:33:53,473
Step 4: F is the extra sum of squares over r, divided by the residual mean square of the full model. Under the null, it has an F distribution with r and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

141
00:33:53,923 --> 00:34:04,803
Step 5: find the p-value, or compare with the critical value. Reject the null if the p-value is less than alpha. Then say the conclusion in words.

142
00:34:05,253 --> 00:34:19,375
Slides 24 and 25 ask a type D question: is fishpart associated with MeHg, in the presence of weight? Pause the video and try it. The bar shows the full model fit.

143
00:34:19,825 --> 00:34:35,612
Step 1 of card D: under the null, all three fishpart coefficients are 0. So r is q, which is 3. It is also 133 minus 130, the difference of the residual degrees of freedom.

144
00:34:36,062 --> 00:34:45,193
Step 2: the reduced model leaves out the three fishpart columns. It is MeHg on weight only. Its bar is on top.

145
00:34:45,643 --> 00:35:08,960
Step 3: the extra sum of squares is 263.742 minus 187.166, which is 76.576. The drop in S S Res, 904.605 minus 828.030, gives the same number.

146
00:35:09,410 --> 00:35:30,039
Step 4: divide by 3 to get 25.525. Divide by the residual mean square of the full model, 6.3695. F is 4.007. The blue line marks it on the F distribution with 3 and 130 degrees of freedom.

147
00:35:30,489 --> 00:35:54,916
Step 5: the 95 percent quantile is 2.674. The p-value is 0.009128, less than 0.05. We reject the null hypothesis. Fishpart is associated with MeHg, given weight. Video 19 found the same p-value with the Lecture 9 formula.

148
00:35:55,366 --> 00:36:02,086
Slide 25 also shows a table that R prints for two models. Each row is one model.

149
00:36:02,536 --> 00:36:25,043
Step 3 of card D, fit both models. Res dot Df is the residual degrees of freedom: 133 and 130. RSS means residual sum of squares, which is S S Res: 904.61 for the reduced model and 828.03 for the full model.

150
00:36:25,493 --> 00:36:50,538
Sum of Sq is the drop in RSS: 904.605 minus 828.030 is 76.576. R prints the RSS rounded. This is the extra regression sum of squares, because both models have the same S S Total. In the bars, the red box is the same piece.

151
00:36:50,988 --> 00:37:03,703
Steps 4 and 5: Df is r, which is 3. F is 4.0074, and the p-value is 0.009128, as before.

152
00:37:04,153 --> 00:37:21,027
Practice question 2 is type D. The model has four covariates. How would you test beta 1 equals beta 2, and beta 3 equals beta 4? Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card D: write it as C beta equals 0.

153
00:37:21,477 --> 00:37:34,043
Row 1 of C is 0, 1, minus 1, 0, 0. Times beta, it gives beta 1 minus beta 2. Setting it to 0 gives beta 1 equals beta 2.

154
00:37:34,493 --> 00:37:47,527
Row 2 is 0, 0, 0, 1, minus 1. It gives beta 3 minus beta 4 equals 0. The two rows are not multiples of each other, so the rank r is 2.

155
00:37:47,977 --> 00:38:07,305
Step 4: use the F test of slide 33, with r equal to 2. p is 4, so n minus p plus 1 is n minus 5. Under the null, F has an F distribution with 2 and n minus 5 degrees of freedom. Next: step 2, the reduced model.

156
00:38:07,755 --> 00:38:19,382
Step 2 of card D, slide 39. Pause the video and try it. Start from the full model. Under the null, beta 2 and beta 4 change, so they are marked.

157
00:38:19,832 --> 00:38:33,165
Put beta 2 equals beta 1, and beta 4 equals beta 3, into the model. The columns x 1 and x 2 now have the same coefficient, and so do x 3 and x 4.

158
00:38:33,615 --> 00:38:52,111
Group the terms. The reduced model is beta 0, plus beta 1 times x i 1 plus x i 2, plus beta 3 times x i 3 plus x i 4. In the picture, the blue columns add to one column, and the orange columns add to another.

159
00:38:52,561 --> 00:39:09,607
Steps 3 and 4: fit the reduced model on the two new covariates. The F statistic uses the extra regression sum of squares over r equal to 2, and the residual mean square of the full model, with n minus 5 degrees of freedom.

160
00:39:10,057 --> 00:39:30,643
Practice question 3 uses the same model. How would you test C beta equals 0 for this C, and what does it test? Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card D: read the rows. C has 3 rows. Each row has 1 in the column of beta 1, and minus 1 in one other column.

161
00:39:31,093 --> 00:39:37,621
Row 1 gives beta 1 minus beta 2 equals 0, so beta 1 equals beta 2.

162
00:39:38,071 --> 00:39:41,250
Row 2 gives beta 1 equals beta 3.

163
00:39:41,700 --> 00:39:45,028
Row 3 gives beta 1 equals beta 4.

164
00:39:45,478 --> 00:40:00,091
Together: beta 1 equals beta 2 equals beta 3 equals beta 4. The null says all four slopes are equal. The three rows are not linear combinations of each other, so the rank r is 3.

165
00:40:00,541 --> 00:40:11,848
Step 2 of card D, slide 41. Pause the video and try it. Start from the full model. Beta 2, beta 3 and beta 4 change, so they are marked.

166
00:40:12,298 --> 00:40:22,090
Put beta 2, beta 3 and beta 4 equal to beta 1 into the model. All four covariate columns now have the same coefficient.

167
00:40:22,540 --> 00:40:38,455
Group the terms. The reduced model is beta 0 plus beta 1 times the sum of the four covariates. So it is a simple linear regression on the new covariate x tilde i, the sum of x i 1 to x i 4.

168
00:40:38,905 --> 00:40:50,041
Steps 3 and 4: use the F test with r equal to 3. Under the null, F has an F distribution with 3 and n minus 5 degrees of freedom.

169
00:40:50,491 --> 00:41:11,333
The last type D problem is Tutorial 3, question 1 k. The model fit n l adds experience and experience squared to the district size. beta 3 and beta 4 are their coefficients. What does the test of beta 3 equals beta 4 equals 0 mean? Pause the video and try it.

170
00:41:11,783 --> 00:41:30,557
Step 1 of card D: C has two rows, one for beta 3 and one for beta 4, so r is 2. The posted solution says what it tests: no association between mean salary and experience, given size, against a linear or quadratic association.

171
00:41:31,007 --> 00:41:43,337
Step 2: the reduced model leaves out experience and experience squared. That is salary on size only, which is fit 1 of the same tutorial. Its bar is on top.

172
00:41:43,787 --> 00:42:04,011
Step 3: both fits use the same y, so we can work in units of S S Total. The extra S S Reg is the difference of the two R squared values: 0.5784 minus 0.3909, which is 0.1875.

173
00:42:04,461 --> 00:42:20,248
Step 4: divide the top and the bottom of F by S S Total. The top becomes the R squared difference over r. The bottom becomes 1 minus R squared of the full model, over its residual degrees of freedom.

174
00:42:20,698 --> 00:42:39,514
Put in the numbers: 0.1875 over 2 is 0.09375. 0.4216 over 320 is 0.0013175. F is 71.16.

175
00:42:39,964 --> 00:43:00,423
Step 5: the 95 percent quantile of F with 2 and 320 degrees of freedom is 3.024. 71.16 is much larger. We reject the null hypothesis. Experience is associated with mean salary, given the district size.

176
00:43:00,873 --> 00:43:15,081
Section 5. Slide 34 lists the tests we now have. t tests are for one number: one coefficient, beta j equals theta 0, or one linear combination of coefficients.

177
00:43:15,531 --> 00:43:31,829
F tests are for several restrictions at the same time: a joint null, beta star equals 0, or a general linear hypothesis, C beta equals 0. Each F test compares a full model and a reduced model.

178
00:43:32,279 --> 00:43:40,962
The test of the entire model is one case: X star has all p covariates, and the reduced model has only the intercept.

179
00:43:41,412 --> 00:43:56,324
To recap. Card A shows the decomposition: split each deviation, square and add, use the facts, and name the sums. S S Total equals S S Reg plus S S Res, and each has a matrix form.

180
00:43:56,774 --> 00:44:12,667
Card B fills the ANOVA table: the degrees of freedom add, the sums of squares add, each mean square is S S over d f, F is the ratio of the mean squares, and R squared is S S Reg over S S Total.

181
00:44:13,117 --> 00:44:25,320
Card C tests the entire model: all covariate coefficients are 0, F has p and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom, then the p-value and the conclusion.

182
00:44:25,770 --> 00:44:41,087
Card D tests a group, or any C beta equals 0: write C, fit the reduced model, take the extra sum of squares, compute F with r degrees of freedom, and conclude. This is the end of video 21.
